Never probe for pthread_setname_np.

pthread_setname_np arrived in glibc 2.12 and is exported by libpthread, not
libc. zmq_check_pthread_setname() is a *compile* check
(builds/cmake/Modules/ZMQSourceRunChecks.cmake), so it only tests whether the
cross toolchain's pthread.h declares the symbol -- which it does regardless of
what the target actually ships. On a glibc 2.11 device the result is
ZMQ_HAVE_PTHREAD_SETNAME_2 in platform.hpp, src/thread.cpp:371 calling it, and
libzmq.so.5 carrying an undefined pthread_setname_np@GLIBC_2.12 that
/lib/libpthread-2.11.1.so cannot satisfy. It is a lazy (JUMP_SLOT) binding, so
this is a crash on the first thread rename rather than a load failure.

`make bincheck` does not catch it: bincheck.py only masks over-new symbols for
sonames matching `libc.so`.

thread_t::setThreadName() compiles to a no-op with all four variants off, which
is what every non-Linux, non-BSD build already gets. Naming is debug-only.
--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -732,7 +732,13 @@
 # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 if (NOT MSVC)
   # Compilation checks
-  zmq_check_pthread_setname()
+  # pthread_setname_np is glibc >= 2.12 and lives in libpthread; the probe only
+  # tests the toolchain headers, so it succeeds on an older target. Force all
+  # four variants off instead of calling it -- as cache entries, because a
+  # merely-absent call would let a stale cached TRUE through unnoticed.
+  foreach(_V SETNAME_1 SETNAME_2 SETNAME_3 SET_NAME)
+    set(ZMQ_HAVE_PTHREAD_${_V} FALSE CACHE INTERNAL "disabled: target glibc < 2.12" FORCE)
+  endforeach()
   zmq_check_pthread_setaffinity()
   # Execution checks
   if(NOT CMAKE_CROSSCOMPILING)
